<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinjar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinjar is a town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> at the southern foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinjar-mountains">Sinjar Mountains</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a fortified city on the route from Mosul to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, held by Zengid atabegs in the twelfth and early thirteenth centuries. The region is a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazidis">Yazidis</a>, who suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> there at the hands of the Islamic State in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinjar.jpg" alt="Sinjar" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amjad.sahep · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinjar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Seljuk defeats in Khorasan and the death of Ahmad Sanjar in 1157 the Zengids remained Seljuk vassals in name but became essentially independent, and the house branched into lines at Mosul, Aleppo, Damascus, <b>Sinjar</b>, al-Jazira and Shahrazur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 14th century the Yazidi lands formed seven centres, each with its sinjaq: Lalish and Sheikhan, Shingal (<b>Sinjar</b>), Hakkari, the lands around Siirt and Mardin, Aleppo and Afrin, Tabriz, and Serhed around Kars.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
